A||||6||theogre||Accell Coils for DIS (PN# 140017)||||4||
Z||000000||theogre||03-26-2000||01:08 PM||||Accell 140017<P>Why:<P>At nearly 150,000 miles I figure the coils are just old. The Accell coils are about the same cost as OEM depending on where you get them.<P><B>Warning:</B><P>These coils DO NOT meet OEM specification for mounting. To properly mount this coil it is required that a guide post on the PIM be removed. Care must be taken not to damage the PIM. The post must be trimmed flush to the face of the PIM. There is one post per coil in the Fiero PIM. Other PIM’s may have 2 posts per coil.<P><B>read all warnings in posts below this one.</B> Falure to properly handle and install parts on the "brick" can result in a ruined or damaged PIM. The PIM is 100-200$us to replace.<P>Install:<P>The firing order is 1-4-2-3 counted from the bottom terminal.<P>The bolts that retain the coils are not studs. They are carriage bolts that pass thru the mounting base, the PIM and then the coils. The bolt heads must be held in place in the mounting base while removing and  installing the coil retainer nuts.<P>Dielectric grease should be packed into the primary terminals of the coils before mounting to prevent entry of dirt and water. Dielectric grease should also be spread over the secondary terminals where the brass exits the plastic.<P>Do not over tighten the coil bolts or you could damage the PIM.<P>Result:<P>The new coils look nice. Idle is a little smoother. I’ll have to see what it’s like after the ECM learns the new data.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-28-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000001||TheFiero64||03-26-2000||07:03 PM||||I had the accell coils in my 88 when it was a 4. It ran smoother & started better. I also liked the yellow color||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||03-26-2000||09:02 PM||||Just slightly off topic: I have a '94ish set of DIS coils off a 70,000 mile v6 that I will give away if somebody wants them. Email me because I might not check this topic for a few days.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-27-2000||11:07 PM||||<B>Warning!¡!</B><P>Also read the thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002921.html" TARGET=_blank>HOW TO: The GM DIS brick.</A> The Accell coils draw more power from the PIM than the OEM ones do. If the PIM and/or crank position sensor have any problem at all you'll find out in some very nasty ways.<P>If there is oil on the back of the coil mounting plate the entire brick MUST be serviced. You likely have an oil leak either from the top brick anchor bolts or from the crank position sensor.<P>Finally, If after installing these coils you are driving allong and the car starts acting up, or dies and won't restart and stay running,,,,, you likely have a PIM that cannot take the extra load of the Accell coils. My own car for example has an aftermarket "Standard" brand PIM. It over heats and craps out with the Accell coils. (I've come to the conclusion the PIM is marginal at best. It will be replaced.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||03-28-2000||08:14 PM||||Ogre ain't happy. Looks like one or both the new coils are bad. replaced the PIM and the crank pos sensor, they really needed it any way, but the car still won't run right with the Accell coils.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||03-30-2000||01:11 AM||||Are the stock coils the Magnavox coils with the outputs on opposite sides?||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-30-2000||07:22 PM||||No. All cast iron 2.5's using DIS are type 2 with the posts on the same side.<P>presently I'm going to have to replace the Accell coils. I replaced the PIM with a Borg/Warner one and with the Accell coils the car wouldn't even run. I put the OEM coils back on and it runs great. ||||reg||1
